# Building Smaller Websites

It is easy for a small website to become complicated.

A few pages become a framework. A little interactivity becomes a client-side application. A handful of styles become a design system with dozens of components.

Sometimes that complexity is justified. Often, it is not.

For personal sites, portfolios, documentation, and many content-focused projects, plain HTML and CSS can take you surprisingly far.

## Start With the Content

I like to begin by asking what the page actually needs to communicate.

If the answer is a heading, a few paragraphs, some links, and a handful of images, then the implementation should probably remain close to that structure.

HTML already provides headings, paragraphs, lists, links, figures, navigation, and articles. Using those elements directly keeps the page understandable both to browsers and to people reading the source.

## Fewer Moving Parts

Every dependency introduces another thing that can change.

Libraries are updated. APIs are deprecated. Build configurations evolve. Tooling that felt convenient today may become maintenance work several years from now.

A smaller stack does not eliminate maintenance, but it reduces the number of places where problems can appear.

This is one reason I enjoy building content-oriented tools. The source can stay pleasant to write while the final output remains ordinary HTML and CSS.

I wrote more about that idea in [Plain Text as a Publishing Format](::plain-text-publishing).

## Performance Comes Naturally

The easiest kilobyte to optimize is the one you never send.

A document that begins as semantic HTML and uses a modest stylesheet tends to perform well without requiring a separate performance project.

That does not mean every site should avoid JavaScript. It means JavaScript can be added where it provides something useful rather than being assumed as the foundation.

## Build What the Site Needs

There is no prize for using the smallest possible technology stack.

There is value, however, in choosing a stack proportional to the problem.

For many websites, simplicity is not a limitation. It is a feature.